WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:04.838
When I was a kid, magnetic levitation trains,
maglevs, were going to be the future.

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:07.707
Television shows would film
breathless reports on the latest trials

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:09.342
in Germany,
or here in Japan,

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:13.847
promising unbelievably fast and smooth travel
between faraway cities.

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:17.450
But decades later, the only actual
high-speed maglev service in the world

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:18.818
is in Shanghai, China.

00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:20.820
Which is fast, but it's almost a gimmick.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.122
It only goes 30km.

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:24.391
It loses enormous amounts of money
every year.

00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:25.992
Not many people use it.

00:00:25.992 --> 00:00:28.028
I thought that maglev
was one of those technologies

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:31.631
like Concorde, that we thought
with the future until they weren't,

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:35.201
and now there’s just abandoned test tracks
and the dreams of the past.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:38.872
Which is why I was very surprised
when my local producer here in Japan

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:42.709
asked me if I wanted to take a ride
on the new maglev.

00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:45.845
Because it turns out
Japan has had a test track for years.

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:48.014
It carries passengers,
because every so often

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:51.718
they will open their tests to the public,
with a lottery for tickets.

00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:56.022
And this test track is already longer
than the entire Shanghai maglev system.

00:00:56.022 --> 00:01:00.493
The plan is that it'll be part of a
285km route, taking 40 minutes,

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:03.563
which is less than half the time
of even the bullet train,

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:06.433
with a cruising speed just over 500km/h.

00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:10.070
90% of that track will be in tunnels,
and that's under construction now.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.638
Forgive me for getting excited,

00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:15.442
but the future appears to be arriving,
and I'm going to take a ride on it.

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:19.112
But first, the team here said
they'd let me stand by the track,

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:23.516
as the maglev goes past, at speed.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:44.404
And that's the maglev. Wow.

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:48.842
So it's going to accelerate

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:53.012
and then they’re going to bring it back,
at full speed, into that tunnel,

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:54.814
500 km/h.

00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:02.956
That accelerates so quickly.

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:07.060
I don't know if that comes
across on camera, but a normal train

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.963
would have started so much slower
than that.

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:14.734
It's already doing,
what, 30, 40 km/h in a few seconds?

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:18.238
That would have pushed
you back in your seat. Wow.

00:02:18.238 --> 00:02:22.842
Okay. Now we wait for it to come that way.

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:47.634
Won’t lie... little bit nervous.

00:02:47.634 --> 00:02:52.138
I know I'm safe, but there's a reason I'm
not allowed behind this line.

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:55.708
It's about maybe a kilometer

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:58.578
to that tunnel mouth, maybe a little less.

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:00.713
And the train's now coming towards us.

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:08.655
500 km/h, which means it's going
about 1km every 8 seconds.

00:03:08.655 --> 00:03:10.290
It's nearly here. Apparently.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.525
They've just told me it's nearly
here, and I can't hear a thing.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:14.661
Oh, I can see a light.

00:03:14.661 --> 00:03:16.896
I can see the train coming.
I can see headlights.

00:03:16.896 --> 00:03:20.600
All right, how fast is 500 km/h?

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:30.677
I hope that came across on camera.

00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:35.315
‘Cos you hear nothing,
and it looks so far away,

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:38.818
and it looks so unthreatening.

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:43.356
And then it is there
and this wall of sound hits you.

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:46.593
That's incredible.

00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:49.262
I don't have words.

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:51.965
It's my job to have words for a moment
like this, and I don't!

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:54.000
All right.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.802
It's going to come to a stop.

00:03:55.802 --> 00:03:57.470
I'm going to get on it.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.305
We're going to ride at 500 km/h.

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:46.886
It smells new.

00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:49.756
There’s new plastics
and fabrics in the air.

00:04:50.757 --> 00:04:51.624
Okay.

00:04:52.392 --> 00:04:53.359
Here we go.

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:58.464
The acceleration is noticeable.

00:04:58.464 --> 00:05:02.335
Like, I got pushed back in my seat there,

00:05:02.335 --> 00:05:05.338
in a way that I think
I've only felt on planes.

00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:09.642
It's not full-plane-takeoff,
but it's still way stronger

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:14.547
than anything that I’ve felt in a train
anywhere else.

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:17.483
We're already approaching 100 km/h.

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:19.819
That is...

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:23.623
100, and we’re still
acclerating.

00:05:25.124 --> 00:05:27.293
130.

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:29.729
So the wheels are going to come up soon,
we're going to hit 150 km/h,

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:31.431
the wheels are going to come up...

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:35.835
And the noise goes away,

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:37.837
and it's so much smoother.

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:41.174
200 km/h.

00:05:41.174 --> 00:05:44.477
And we're still accelerating.

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:51.084
The only reference I've got now
is the big screen,

00:05:51.084 --> 00:05:54.854
which is just counting down
how long until the end of the track.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:01.561
It's a little bit bouncy, but nothing
you wouldn't expect on a regular train.

00:06:03.262 --> 00:06:04.997
Okay. 300 km/h.

00:06:04.997 --> 00:06:09.902
We're at 'continental Europe high speed train'
now, and we're still accelerating.

00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:11.404
I'm still being pushed back in my seat.

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:14.907
Not dramatically,
but it's still noticeable.

00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:20.747
I've never experienced
this much acceleration for this long.

00:06:20.747 --> 00:06:22.582
And we're still going.

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:20.006
480. 490.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:23.476
And we ease off the acceleration.

00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:24.811
That's 500 km/h.

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:29.582
That's the fastest I've ever
traveled on land.

00:07:29.582 --> 00:07:32.251
And it feels... like a train!

00:07:32.985 --> 00:07:35.521
It is a little bit louder
than you'd expect from a train,

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:38.124
but it's way less than an aircraft.

00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:39.158
You can still have a conversation, there’s

00:07:39.158 --> 00:07:42.094
a bit of noise in the background,
but it's still doing okay.

00:07:42.528 --> 00:07:43.496
But there's nothing uncomfortable

00:07:43.496 --> 00:07:45.431
about this.
I was worried that at this speed

00:07:45.431 --> 00:07:47.700
I was going to feel like
I was being jolted about.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:48.668
But this...

00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:50.570
this feels like a train!

00:08:33.246 --> 00:08:35.147
So will it work this time?

00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:38.518
Because there is a long history
of breathless British people

00:08:38.518 --> 00:08:41.521
traveling to other countries
and getting excited about maglevs

00:08:41.521 --> 00:08:45.358
and telling the English-speaking world
about the future that never comes.

00:08:45.358 --> 00:08:49.562
This feels like a video that the BBC
could have filmed in the 70s or 80s.

00:08:49.562 --> 00:08:53.165
And the original opening date of 2027
has already been pushed back,

00:08:53.165 --> 00:08:54.767
the new date is still uncertain.

00:08:54.767 --> 00:08:58.671
One local government keeps raising
objections to the construction.

00:08:58.671 --> 00:09:00.673
The future is not here yet,
and we're not going

00:09:00.706 --> 00:09:03.442
be seeing one of these in Europe
any time soon.

00:09:03.442 --> 00:09:05.611
But the track is being built.

00:09:05.611 --> 00:09:07.346
The technology does work.

00:09:07.346 --> 00:09:08.915
And it looks like the economics are sound,

00:09:08.915 --> 00:09:13.052
because there's clearly demand
for traveling between two major cities.

00:09:13.052 --> 00:09:17.056
In a few years’ time,
we might finally get to ride the maglev

00:09:17.056 --> 00:09:21.627
that the world was promised
all those years ago.

00:09:21.627 --> 00:09:24.997
We are braking now
and it is still really strong.

00:09:26.632 --> 00:09:28.034
One last thing.

00:09:28.034 --> 00:09:31.237
They stopped the train
in the middle of a curve.

00:09:32.138 --> 00:09:35.942
When I was coming through this at
500 km/h, it felt perfectly level to me.

00:09:36.142 --> 00:09:39.045
I felt nothing weird,
but it turns out that actually...

00:09:39.045 --> 00:09:41.180
the whole train is tilted ten degrees!

00:09:41.213 --> 00:09:44.517
I'm having to brace myself
to stop sliding down the carpet.

00:09:45.251 --> 00:09:49.188
Thank you very much to everyone from JR,
who’s standing behind the camera there.

00:09:49.188 --> 00:09:51.691
This was a privilege.
Thank you very, very much.

